[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Sandbox Security, Method Approvals, and CPS Transformation in Jenkins"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Jenkins Pipeline is a powerful tool for defining continuous delivery workflows as code. However, executing arbitrary Groovy scripts in a shared Jenkins environment introduces significant security risks. Jenkins uses a Sandbox security model, CPS (Continuation Passing Style) transformation, and a Method Approval system to balance flexibility and security."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Understanding CPS Transformation"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"When you write a Jenkinsfile using the declarative or scripted pipeline, Jenkins does not execute your Groovy code directly using standard Groovy runtimes. Instead, it uses a custom compiler to parse your script into Continuation Passing Style (CPS)."},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"CPS transformation allows Jenkins to pause, serialize, and resume pipeline execution at any point. This is crucial for handling long-running operations, Jenkins restarts, and agent disconnections without losing pipeline state. However, it also means your Groovy code is intercepted and rewritten to track every method call and variable assignment."},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. The Jenkins Sandbox Security Model"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"By default, pipelines run inside the Groovy Sandbox. The sandbox acts as a security boundary, restricting scripts to a safe subset of APIs, methods, and constructors. If a pipeline attempts to call a method that is deemed unsafe or not explicitly whitelisted, the sandbox intercepts the call and fails the build."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"node {
    stage('Sandbox Test') {
        // This safe method runs without issue
        echo 'Hello from the sandbox'

        // This will trigger a sandbox violation unless approved
        // def file = new File('/etc/passwd')
    }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Handling Method Approvals"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"When a pipeline script attempts to use a method or constructor that is not whitelisted by default, the build pauses or fails, and an entry is logged in the In-process Script Approval queue. Jenkins administrators can review and approve these signatures."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Navigate to Manage Jenkins in your dashboard."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Click on In-process Script Approval."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Review pending signatures, such as method java.lang.String toUpperCase."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Click Approve to allow the method execution globally across pipelines."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Disabling the Sandbox for Trusted Pipelines"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"For trusted repositories or pipelines requiring advanced administrative capabilities, you can uncheck the 'Use Groovy Sandbox' option in the pipeline configuration. When the sandbox is disabled, the script runs with full Jenkins master privileges (often referred to as 'Master-level permissions')."},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"WARNING: Disabling the sandbox means any user with permission to modify the Jenkinsfile can execute arbitrary system commands on the Jenkins controller. Always ensure strict branch source permissions and review changes carefully when disabling the sandbox."},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always keep pipelines inside the Groovy Sandbox unless absolute master-level access is required."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Encapsulate complex logic into Shared Libraries, where approvals can be managed centrally."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Regularly audit the In-process Script Approval page to remove unused or suspicious method signatures."}]
